# Whole /private/ section: noindex (covers the section index + any future tool
# page). The meet-recorder block below adds its page-specific headers on top.
/private/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/private/tools/meet-recorder/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(self), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'

# Thanks page (issue #43): thin confirmation page, keep it out of the index.
/community/asians-gingers-in-tech/thanks/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Newsletter double opt-in (issue #56): the whole section. Two thin confirmation
# pages plus the section landing, none of which belongs in an index.
# The glob covers /newsletter/ itself as well as its children, because fnmatch
# lets the `*` match empty.
#
# What this rule does and does NOT do (corrected at Ralph round 22 Tier 3, which
# found the previous wording wrong in three separate ways):
# It is the noindex directive Cloudflare serves. It is NOT what exempts these
# pages from the social-card guard -- their own `sitemap: disable: true`
# frontmatter already does that, because check_social_cards.py short-circuits on
# it several lines BEFORE it ever consults these rules. So do not "simplify" by
# deleting that frontmatter on the theory this rule covers it; removing it
# reopens the round-6 sitemap/feed leak.
# Also: /newsletter/ has its own content/newsletter/_index.md, so it is a
# declared section (social-card Check C), not an auto-generated one (Check D).
# That _index.md exists precisely to stop auto-generation.
/newsletter/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# The 404 template (blog-priv#64) is also reachable as an ordinary page:
# Cloudflare serves it at /404.html, which 308-redirects to /404 and answers
# HTTP 200 with the full "Page not found" HTML. Left alone that is an indexable
# soft-200, which is the exact defect blog-priv#64 removed from every dead URL,
# so it would have been relocated to one fixed URL rather than fixed. The 404
# RESPONSES themselves are unaffected: this only tells crawlers not to index the
# page when they reach it by its own address. Asserted by scripts/test_404.py.
/404*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# NO Cache-Control rule for /js/* here, deliberately, and this is measured
# rather than assumed. hoiboy-uk#57 shipped one (`public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate`) to stop a fresh page being served against a stale script.
# It changed NOTHING. Measured live with the rule deployed, and again with it
# removed, the response was `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` both
# times -- byte-identical to /css/main.min.<hash>.css and /favicon.ico, which
# have no rule of any kind. That is Cloudflare Pages' asset default, and a
# per-path Cache-Control does not displace it: Pages JOINS same-name headers
# rather than replacing them, the same behaviour the CSP note below records.
#
# A rule that reads as though it works and does not is worse than no rule, so
# it was removed. The staleness is fixed where it can be fixed, in the URL:
# layouts/_shortcodes/versioned-script.html appends the script's own content
# hash, and a different URL is a different cache key.

# Turnstile (challenges.cloudflare.com) is allowed site-wide in the /* CSP below.
# TWO forms depend on this allowance, so check both before narrowing or removing it:
#   1. the community "Get featured" form, on one page, and
#   2. the newsletter subscribe form in layouts/_partials/footer.html, which renders
#      on nearly every page (everything except legal, private, 404 and the AGIT
#      funnel pages, per that partial's suppression predicate).
# The second one is why "inert on pages with no cf-turnstile widget" is now the
# exception rather than the rule.
#
# Cloudflare Pages JOINS same-name headers across path rules rather than replacing
# them, so a per-path CSP block cannot ADD a source the root policy lacks: the two
# policies are sent as two CSP headers and the browser enforces their intersection,
# dropping the wider source. The root policy therefore carries
# challenges.cloudflare.com.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
